::::

\

To complete the command, Linux puts a `\` before the spaces. This is known as escaping characters. When we put a `\` before a special character, we tell the computer to disregard what it would normally mean. We can also use that in full paths.

``` bash
ls ~/Intro_to_Linux/dir\ with\ spaces
```

It's not the nicest, but it does mean we can look at files that have special characters without having to rename them before working with them. 

